WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.963
One endpoint, one request shape.

2
00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:09.432
Almost everything you will build in this program goes through a single HTTP endpoint: the Messages API.

3
00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:15.344
You send a JSON body describing a conversation, and you get back one assistant message.

4
00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:22.643
Tools, structured outputs, images, and documents are all features of that same request, not separate APIs.

5
00:00:22.923 --> 00:00:26.656
Learn its shape once and the rest of the studio is variations on it.

6
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:29.624
A minimal request has three required fields.

7
00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:34.235
model names the exact model, for example claude-opus-5-5.

8
00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:44.157
max_tokens is a hard ceiling on how many tokens the model may generate in this response; it is a ceiling, not a target, and the model does not try to fill it.

9
00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:51.755
messages is the conversation so far: a list of turns, each with a role of user or assistant and some content.

10
00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:53.976
The first turn must be from the user.

11
00:00:54.256 --> 00:00:56.112
Two more fields matter on day one.

12
00:00:56.392 --> 00:01:04.136
system is a top-level field (not a message) that holds your standing instructions: the role, the rules, the output format.

13
00:01:04.416 --> 00:01:08.661
And content can be either a plain string or a list of typed content blocks.

14
00:01:08.941 --> 00:01:10.883
A text block is the simple case.

15
00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:18.715
Images, PDF documents, tool calls, and tool results are other block types, and you will meet them in weeks 2 and 3.

16
00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:27.037
If you have used older chat APIs, notice that the system prompt is not a message with role “system” here; it lives in its own field.
